findings.proposal:
Keep ONE lot x time matrix in process: 4 lots x 8,064 buckets as array('f') = 129 KB. Append one column when the pg_cron collect already calls /area-demand/snapshots/collect every 10 minutes, and load the full matrix once at boot with one query. Per grid, derive the 4 distance weights once per facility snapshot (the same math as aggregate_nearby_points, which the docstring already keeps as the reference implementation).
At collect time, precompute per grid:
- a (weekend, KST minute-of-day) median baseline table (2 x 1440 x uint16 = 5.8 KB per grid; about 3.5 MB for 600 facility grids, or 10x less at 10-minute resolution);
- the last 9 levels for the recent-adjustment term;
- the quality result once per day.
Scoring then becomes an O(1) lookup plus the existing recent_adjustment formula (forecast_service.py:452-468). The result is exactly the same as today between collects, because no new points exist until the next collect. The single source of truth becomes the lot matrix.

New module `parking_history.py` holding:
- `epoch_us: array('q')` (int64 microseconds, so isoformat() round-trips exactly);
- `total`, `avail`: uint16 matrices of shape T x L, with a validity mask;
- per-column lot id, lat, lng (a new column if a lot's coordinates ever change);
- precomputed per-row KST minute, weekend flag and KST day ordinal;
- `version = last bucket_at`.
Boot: load once with a new additive read RPC `area_demand_lot_matrix(p_source, p_since)` that returns columnar arrays (about 300KB). Until the migration is applied, fall back to the narrow embedded select.
Write path: the collector (routers/area_demand.py:150-158, after `_persist_snapshot` succeeds) appends one row (O(L)), trims rows older than 56 days and bumps `version`. It is a single writer: pg_cron, Actions and manual runs all go through the API.
Safety net: on read, if `now - version > 15 min`, do a cheap tail-sync (`bucket_at > version`, a few rows). This covers deploy overlap and restarts.
Read path, `forecast(lat, lng, arrival)`:
- L haversines give per-lot weights, then level = sum(occ x w) / sum(w) over all rows: 0.2ms numpy / 3.5ms pure Python, bit-identical to `aggregate_nearby_points` (verified);
- eligible mask by minute/weekend/cutoff, then `statistics.median`: 0.05ms, same baseline and sample count as today (verified);
- backtest quality looked up by (grid, version), see the backtest finding.
Caches become keyed by data version instead of wall-clock TTL. The estimator, estimated report, parking_derived, calibration hour-shape and reliability 'latest lots' should read the same store: one source of truth.
